# ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A Windows self-hosted runner joined the pool on 2026-09-16. Git on Windows
# checks out text with CRLF by default, and a large part of this repo's test
# suite PARSES TEXT: tests/support/sql-migrations.ts reads every file in
# supabase/migrations/ and splits it into lines, and several suites then match
# line-anchored patterns against the result.
#
# With CRLF every one of those lines carries a trailing \r, every anchored
# pattern misses, and the suites fail with assertions about policies and
# guards that say nothing whatsoever about line endings. Run 35142420876:
# Typecheck passed, Tests failed, and the failures were exactly the suites that
# read SQL.
#
# So line endings are pinned here rather than left to whichever machine picked
# up the job. The parser was ALSO made CRLF-tolerant in the same commit -- this
# file makes the checkout deterministic, that change makes it not matter.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one thing that genuinely needs CRLF: cmd.exe can
# mis-parse a LF-only .bat. gradlew.bat is the live example here.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Leave binaries alone. `text=auto` detects most of these, but the launcher
# icons and any future keystore are worth being explicit about -- a corrupted
# PNG from newline translation is a confusing thing to debug.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.jks   binary
*.keystore binary
*.p12   binary
*.node  binary
